== Storyline notes ==

* '''The end of Kuroshio.''' The four former members of Kuroshio — Leoni Masua, Ryohei Maeda, KAZUKI, and Daigo Kuroki — were in the same ring together for the first time since Episode 1. The result was not reconciliation but violence. KAZUKI attacked Maeda in their singles match, then attacked Leoni in the main event. Maeda tried to mediate and failed. Kuroki tried to hold the pieces together and failed. When all four brawled after the main event, the message was clear: the Black Tide is dead. Each man now walks alone into Island Invasion — Leoni as champion, KAZUKI as challenger, Maeda as the mediator with nothing left to mediate, Kuroki as the Seigigun enforcer who has lost his brothers.
* '''Kengo's cheeky win.''' Kengo did not pin Leoni. He did not submit him. He won by countout after KAZUKI interfered and brawled with Leoni on the floor. It was the cheapest possible victory — and exactly the kind of win that defines Kengo's leadership. The Cannon Club leader now has a psychological edge heading into Island Invasion, even though he did not beat the Tribal Chief clean. The image of Kengo smiling in the ring while Leoni brawled with KAZUKI on the floor will be the lasting image of the go-home show.
* '''KAZUKI's isolation is complete.''' KAZUKI defeated Maeda cleanly and then attacked Leoni in the main event, costing Leoni the match against Kengo. He no longer distinguishes between friend and enemy — only between himself and the IGO Heavyweight Championship. His promo — "I don't need Kuroshio. I don't need you. I need that title." — was the final severing of the bond. At Fangatapu, he faces Leoni one-on-one, and there is no one left to talk either man down.
* '''Jusuf Muntasir arrives.''' The Baliscan oil-wrestling champion made his professional wrestling debut by interrupting the IGO Intercontinental Championship match between Marcus Vance and Daigo Kuroki. Jusuf — described as a powerfully built man with dark curly hair, a full beard, and intense dark eyes, wearing black compression gear with heavy leather trousers evoking the traditional Baliscan ''quiswa'' — attacked both men without choosing a side. His promo declared professional wrestling a "lie" compared to the traditional grappling of ''mosaraza''. The debut established him as an outsider heel who disrespects both the sport and its champions. The question: who does he target first — Vance, Kuroki, or the entire promotion?
* '''Marcus Vance's organic babyface turn.''' For the fifth consecutive Wednesday night appearance, Vance was in a match-of-the-night candidate — this time against Kuroki. But for the first time, the Cannon Club did not come to his aid when Jusuf attacked. Kengo, Malo, and the Usos watched from the ramp or backstage. Vance was left alone in the ring with Kuroki, both men victim to the debuting Baliscan. The crowd's reaction shifted: boos for Vance's entrance became concern during the attack, and respect after he refused help. Leoni's backstage observation — "Even a cold man can get lonely" — confirmed that the promotion's top heel is beginning to turn into an organic babyface. The question: how will Vance respond to being abandoned by his own faction?
* '''The young guns win the war of attrition.''' Episode 6 was a statement night for NOPW's young talent. Yuto Ono defeated World TV Champion Jin Tanaka in a non-title upset. Tetsuya Yamamoto defeated Junior Tag Champion Danny Viggo in another upset. The Marauder continued his destructive run. The Seigigun developmental system — which produced Kuroshio — is producing again. The message: the next generation is ready, and the Cannon Club's mid-card is not prepared for them.
* '''Cannon Club's title haul under pressure.''' The Cannon Club went into Episode 6 holding four titles and left holding four — but the night exposed cracks. Tanaka lost to Ono (non-title). Viggo lost to Yamamoto. Big Stan and Diallo lost again. Malo lost to Taumafa. Only Kengo's countout win and the Usos' DQ retention were victories, and neither was clean. The faction's depth problem is now a crisis: the title-holders are strong, but the lower-card cannot win without help. At Island Invasion, the Cannon Club may be forced to defend four titles with a roster that cannot win clean matches.
* '''The Vaiahi family war intensifies.''' Taumafa defeated Malo and challenged Kengo directly. Kengo, watching from the ramp, smiled and did not answer. The family hierarchy — Leoni, Kengo, Taumafa — remains unresolved. At Fangatapu, all three will be on the same card, and the family tension will be impossible to avoid.
* '''The Ogasawara Arena sellout.''' The 9,000-seat arena sold out in 14 hours — the fastest sellout in Wednesday night history, breaking Okayama's 18-hour record. The step from Nagayama (4,200) to Kurosaki (6,500) to Tosa (6,000) to Naruto (7,500) to Okayama (8,500) to Ogasawara (9,000) showed the product's growth across six different markets. Wednesday nights are now a touring phenomenon. The next stop is Fangatapu, Vaiahi Islands — the biggest event of the NOPW calendar.

