== Aftermath ==

* The Kuroshio bond officially ended at Episode 6. The four former members — Leoni Masua, Ryohei Maeda, KAZUKI, and Daigo Kuroki — brawled in the ring after the main event, with KAZUKI attacking both Maeda and Leoni. The Black Tide, which disbanded in 2017 as a developmental stable, is now dead as a brotherhood. Each man enters Island Invasion alone: Leoni as champion, KAZUKI as challenger, Maeda as a mediator with no one left to mediate, and Kuroki as a Seigigun soldier who has lost his former brothers.
* Kengo Vaiahi defeated Leoni Masua by countout in the main event — a cheeky, strategic victory that gave the Cannon Club leader psychological momentum without damaging Leoni's championship status. The win came because KAZUKI interfered and brawled with Leoni on the floor, allowing the referee to count Leoni out. The image of Kengo smiling while Leoni and KAZUKI fought will be central to the Island Invasion promotional package.
* KAZUKI defeated Ryohei Maeda cleanly and then attacked Leoni in the main event, completing his isolation. His promo — "I don't need Kuroshio. I don't need you. I need that title." — was the final statement. At Fangatapu, he faces Leoni one-on-one for the IGO Heavyweight Championship, and there is no bond left to complicate the match.
* Jusuf Muntasir made his professional wrestling debut by interrupting the IGO Intercontinental Championship match between Marcus Vance and Daigo Kuroki. The Baliscan oil wrestler attacked both men and declared professional wrestling a "lie" compared to the traditional grappling of mosaraza. The debut established him as an outsider heel with no faction allegiance. The question heading into Island Invasion: who does Jusuf target first, and does he challenge Vance for the Intercontinental Championship?
* Marcus Vance's fifth consecutive match-of-the-night candidate ended in a no contest due to Jusuf's attack. More importantly, the Cannon Club did not come to Vance's aid — Kengo, Malo, and the Usos watched or stayed away. The crowd's reaction shifted from heat to concern to respect, and Leoni's backstage observation confirmed that Vance is beginning an organic babyface turn. The coolest heel in NOPW may be becoming the most sympathetic man in the promotion.
* The young guns of NOPW had a statement night. Yuto Ono defeated World TV Champion Jin Tanaka in a non-title upset. Tetsuya Yamamoto defeated Junior Tag Champion Danny Viggo in another upset. The Marauder continued his destructive run. The Seigigun developmental system is producing a new generation ready to challenge the established order.
* The Cannon Club went into Episode 6 holding four titles and left holding four, but the night exposed the faction's depth problem. Tanaka, Viggo, Big Stan, Diallo, and Malo all lost. Only Kengo's countout win and the Usos' DQ retention were victories, and neither was clean. At Island Invasion, the faction may be forced to defend four championships with a mid-card that cannot win without interference.
* Taumafa Vaiahi retained the Strong Openweight Championship against Malo and challenged Kengo directly. The Vaiahi family war — Leoni, Kengo, Taumafa — remains unresolved and will be a major subplot at Island Invasion.
* Diego Reyes and Haruto Kimura's tag title match against the Usos ended in a disqualification after Reyes used a steel chair. The Usos retained, but the post-match brawl established that the Island Invasion tag title match will be a violent, personal contest.
* The Ogasawara Arena sellout (9,000) in 14 hours was the fastest in Wednesday night history. The product has grown from Nagayama (4,200) to Kurosaki (6,500) to Tosa (6,000) to Naruto (7,500) to Okayama (8,500) to Ogasawara (9,000) across six consecutive markets. The next stop is Fangatapu, Vaiahi Islands — the biggest event of the NOPW calendar.

